From 2cfac6392852a536452f50d986b1052fbddb981e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Igor Barynov <igorbarinov@fb.com>
Date: Wed, 20 May 2026 07:50:04 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 61/63] [cuttlefish][darwin-host-tools] Fix DNS on eth1: drop
 dns= and disable IPv6 SLIRP
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Summary:
Two changes to QEMU's `-netdev user,id=hostnet1,...` to make DNS work
reliably on device Cuttlefish (and other Cuttlefish products using
SLIRP user-mode networking) on hosts whose network rewrites or blocks
outbound DNS — corp networks, captive portals, etc.

1) Drop `dns=8.8.4.4`. With it, SLIRP advertises 8.8.4.4 to the guest
   via DHCP but does NOT intercept queries to it — they're NAT'd like
   any other UDP traffic. On networks where outbound DNS is hijacked
   (e.g. Meta corp transparently redirects DNS to an internal resolver
   at 192.168.96.53), the response comes back with a source IP that
   doesn't match the original destination, and Android's resolver
   silently drops it. Without `dns=`, SLIRP falls back to its internal
   DNS forwarder at gateway+1 (10.0.1.3), which forwards via the host's
   resolver and rewrites the response source IP back to 10.0.1.3 — so
   Android always sees a reply from the address it queried, regardless
   of what the host network does upstream.

2) Add `ipv6=off`. SLIRP's default IPv6 Router Advertisement on this
   netdev advertises fec0::3 as the IPv6 DNS server. Android prefers
   IPv6 and tries fec0::3 first; fec0::3 never answers, adding 5-30s
   of timeout to every lookup before IPv4 fallback. eth1 is IPv4-only
   on Cuttlefish today, so disabling its SLIRP IPv6 is safe.

Verified on device CF emu (<internal-release> ARM64 + QEMU/HVF on macOS arm64, Meta
corp network) via a qemu-system-aarch64 wrapper that rewrote the
hostnet1 netdev arg at exec time, before applying the change to the
host binary. With both edits in place and NO guest-side iptables NAT
rules, DNS resolves and Android reports the network as VALIDATED.
This supersedes the guest-side iptables workaround we'd been carrying.

Test Plan:
Booted device CF emu with this change applied via wrapper, no guest
iptables rules:

  adb shell ip -6 addr show eth1
    -> link-local fe80:: only (no fec0:: addresses)

  adb shell dumpsys connectivity | grep -A2 'eth1' | grep DnsAddresses
    -> DnsAddresses: [ /10.0.1.3 ]   (SLIRP internal forwarder)

  adb shell 'iptables -t nat -L OUTPUT -n -v'
    -> no DNAT rules

  adb shell ping -c 1 8.8.8.8       -> success
  adb shell ping -c 1 google.com    -> success (was: unknown host)
  adb shell nslookup google.com     -> resolves via 10.0.1.3
                                       (-> 142.251.30.102)

  Android reports network state VALIDATED.

Reviewers: achong, tcare, marcjordan, amanbajaj, achalshah, jeffreyeffendy

Reviewed By: achong

Differential Revision: https://phabricator.intern.facebook.com/D106370091

Tags: aarch64
---
 host/libs/vm_manager/qemu_manager.cpp | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/host/libs/vm_manager/qemu_manager.cpp b/host/libs/vm_manager/qemu_manager.cpp
index fedb02f72..f38cb1e77 100644
--- a/host/libs/vm_manager/qemu_manager.cpp
+++ b/host/libs/vm_manager/qemu_manager.cpp
@@ -932,7 +932,13 @@ Result<std::vector<MonitorCommand>> QemuManager::StartCommands(
                             ",dns=127.0.0.1");
 
       qemu_cmd.AddParameter("-netdev");
-      qemu_cmd.AddParameter("user,id=hostnet1,net=10.0.1.1/24,dns=8.8.4.4");
+      // No dns=: fall back to SLIRP's internal DNS forwarder at gateway+1
+      // (10.0.1.3). Setting dns=<external IP> breaks when the host network
+      // hijacks DNS (corp net, captive portals) — response source IP no
+      // longer matches and Android's resolver drops it.
+      // ipv6=off: suppress SLIRP's default fec0::3 IPv6 RA, which Android
+      // prefers and times out on. eth1 is IPv4-only on Cuttlefish.
+      qemu_cmd.AddParameter("user,id=hostnet1,net=10.0.1.1/24,ipv6=off");
 
       if (!config.virtio_mac80211_hwsim()) {
         qemu_cmd.AddParameter("-netdev");
